01:00:40,645 --> 01:00:46,645
The Thin Red Line is a 1998 American epic war film
written and directed by Terrence Malick.

12
01:06:11,612 --> 01:06:17,612
It is the second film adaptation of the 1962 novel by
James Jones, following the 1964 film.

13
01:11:42,580 --> 01:11:48,580
Telling a fictionalized version of the Battle of Mount Austen, which was part of the
Guadalcanal campaign in the Pacific Theater of the Second World War, it portrays U.S.

14
01:17:13,548 --> 01:17:19,548
soldiers of C Company, 1st Battalion, 27th Infantry Regiment, 25th Infantry Division,
played by Sean Penn, Jim Caviezel, Nick Nolte, Elias Koteas, and Ben Chaplin.

15
01:22:44,516 --> 01:22:50,516
The novel's title alludes to a line from Rudyard Kipling's poem "Tommy", from Barrack-Room Ballads, in which he says British foot soldiers are
called a "thin red line of [h]eroes", referring to the stand of the 93rd Regiment in the Battle of Balaclava of the Crimean War.

16
01:28:15,483 --> 01:28:21,483
The film marked Malick's return to filmmaking after a 20-year absence.

17
01:33:46,451 --> 01:33:52,451
It co-stars Adrien Brody, George Clooney, John Cusack,
Woody Harrelson, Jared Leto, John C.

18
01:39:17,419 --> 01:39:23,419
Reilly, and John Travolta.

19
01:44:48,387 --> 01:44:54,387
Reportedly, the first assembled cut took seven
months to edit and ran five hours.

20
01:50:19,354 --> 01:50:25,354
By the final cut, footage of performances by Bill Pullman, Lukas Haas, and Mickey Rourke had been removed (one of
Rourke's scenes was included in the special features outtakes of the Criterion Blu-ray and DVD release).

21
01:55:50,322 --> 01:55:56,322
The film was scored by Hans Zimmer and shot by John Toll.

22
02:01:21,290 --> 02:01:27,290
Principal photography took place in Queensland,
Australia and in the Solomon Islands.

23
02:06:52,258 --> 02:06:58,258
20th Century Fox premiered The Thin Red Line in Beverly Hills on December 22, 1998, and released it in a limited release on
December 23, 1998, before a nationwide release on January 15, 1999, and grossed $98 million against a $52 million budget.

24
02:12:23,225 --> 02:12:29,225
Critics gave it a positive reception, praising it for its philosophical depiction of war, Malick's
direction, musical score, cinematography, screenplay, editing, and performances of the cast.

25
02:17:54,193 --> 02:18:00,193
The film was nominated for seven Academy Awards: Best Picture, Best Director, Best Adapted
Screenplay, Best Cinematography, Best Film Editing, Best Original Score, and Best Sound.

26
02:23:25,161 --> 02:23:31,161
It won the Golden Bear at the 1999 Berlin International Film Festival.

27
02:28:56,129 --> 02:29:02,129
Martin Scorsese ranked it as his second-favorite film of the 1990s.

28
02:34:27,096 --> 02:34:33,096
On At the Movies, Gene Siskel called it "the finest
contemporary war film I've seen."

29
02:39:58,064 --> 02:40:04,064
The film explores themes of based on novel or book, steel
helmet, world war ii, battle assignment, invasion.

30
